# A handle over a struct that C names with two words (defect 037, panels 151
# and 152; closed 2026-09-15).
#
# **Until this case ran, the binding could not be written.** `record Probe tag
# probe` against a header that declares `struct probe` and gives it no typedef
# emitted `probe *`, clang refused it — *must use 'struct' tag to refer to type
# 'probe'* — and the author got, in turn, `internal error: compiling the
# generated C failed` at exit 2 (until panel 152's first half landed) and then
# `error[ffi_tag_needs_struct]`, an honest refusal with no fix, because a `tag`
# holds one word and the language had no way to write the second. `netdb.h`'s
# `struct addrinfo` is that shape, and so is every linked list in every C
# header.
#
# **What closes it is clang's own sentence, obeyed and not guessed.** The
# emitter still writes the author's word. When clang refuses it with that exact
# message — which it produces only when the bare word names a struct tag of
# that spelling — `cli/produce.hero` learns the tag and compiles once more with
# `struct probe *` where `probe *` was. Two sittings refused the two ways of
# knowing in advance: a spelling read from the header can no longer disagree
# with the header (panel 151), and one bit — does `struct X` compile? — says yes
# for a misspelled X too (panel 152). So the author's word is compiled first,
# every refusal of it survives, and only the qualifier the header demands is
# supplied.
#
# Two tags, so the round learns both from one refusal; both marks, so the
# handles are counted and given back like any other.

extern "fixedbugs-a-tag-that-needs-struct.h"
    record Probe tag probe
    record Gauge tag gauge
    function probe_open(n: i64) -> Probe acquires probe_close
    function probe_value(p: Probe) -> i64
    function probe_close(p: Probe consumes)
    function gauge_open(n: i64) -> Gauge acquires gauge_close
    function gauge_read(g: Gauge) -> i64
    function gauge_close(g: Gauge consumes)

function main()
    p = probe_open(n: 7)
    g = gauge_open(n: 35)
    print(probe_value(p) + gauge_read(g))
    probe_close(p)
    gauge_close(g)
